            <div xml:id="d1102">

                <pb facs="0985" n="978" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg978"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">700.0011 Pacific/4: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-05-17T18:00:00+09:00">May 17, 1937—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received May 17—9:50 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>130. The Foreign Office spokesman today told newspapermen that although the
                    Foreign Office lacks official information about the reported speech of last week
                    in London by Premier Lyons of Australia with regard to a nonaggression pact for
                    nations around the Pacific, as a fundamental idea Japan has no objection to
                    participation. The spokesman added that Japan’s attitude cannot be stated until
                    details of the proposal are learned.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
